preConditionHook:
// SCRATCH A TRADE THAT NEVER WENT ANYWHERE. maxFlatBars bars after the fill,
// the trade's PEAK profit has still not reached flatThreshold ATRs (Grimes
// G10-06).
//
// NOT NumBarsIfLosing, and NOT NumBarsIfBelowProfitThreshold
// either: both read ctx.OpenPositionProfit(), this close's mark. A trade that
// ran two ATRs in favour and gave it all back is "losing" to them and is
// emphatically not flat. This reads ctx.MaxPositionProfit() -- the position's
// PEAK -- so it asks whether the trade ever moved at all, and scales the
// threshold by ATR rather than fixing it in dollars.
//
// NOT FailedScalpExit: that caps the position's age at 1 bar and adds a
// price-level term; this requires the position to have SURVIVED maxFlatBars
// and carries no price term.
//
// ctx.MaxPositionProfit() is VERIFIED as the INTRABAR peak of the favourable
// extreme, net of the entry side's costs, starting at 0 per position and
// restarting through a reversal -- never emulate it.
//
// UNITS: MaxPositionProfit is account currency; flatThreshold * ATR *
// BigPointValue is currency per contract, and BigPointValue is NOT currency
// converted while the profit accessors are -- TradeStation's own
// inconsistency, preserved deliberately, exactly as AtrProfitTarget does it.
//
// SIDES ARE `same`. No MarketPosition guard, matching the NumBarsIf* family;
// the grid must keep maxFlatBars >= 1, since BarsSinceEntry() is 0 while flat.
//
// Every scratch value is a localVariables entry, never a hook-scope
// declaration, so the hooks survive a double placement.
//
// Deepest bar touched: atrLength + 1.
// MultiWalk's WFSafe_AvgTrueRange is a ROLLING accumulator, not a fresh N-term
// sum: seeded with a full sum on the study's first calculated bar and
// thereafter carrying S += TrueRange - TrueRange[Length], re-seeding when the
// length changes. AtrProfitTarget is the reference and carries the
// measurements; the two load-bearing details are reproduced here -- the
// re-seed on a length change (what makes it walkforward safe) and the update
// as TWO statements, because EasyLanguage evaluates S[1] + in - out left to
// right and folding it to S += in - out rounds differently.
if (atrLenState != (int)atrLength) {
    atrSum = 0.0;
    for (int i = 0; i < (int)atrLength; ++i) {
        double hi = high[i] > close[i + 1] ? high[i] : close[i + 1];
        double lo = low[i] < close[i + 1] ? low[i] : close[i + 1];
        atrSum += hi - lo;
    }
    atrLenState = (int)atrLength;
} else {
    const int out = (int)atrLength;
    double hiIn = high[0] > close[1] ? high[0] : close[1];
    double loIn = low[0] < close[1] ? low[0] : close[1];
    double hiOut = high[out] > close[out + 1] ? high[out] : close[out + 1];
    double loOut = low[out] < close[out + 1] ? low[out] : close[out + 1];
    atrSum += hiIn - loIn;
    atrSum -= hiOut - loOut;
}
atrVal = atrSum / (int)atrLength;
